WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.796
External secret stores and platform-managed secret systems centralize protection, access control, auditing, and rotation.

2
00:00:07.796 --> 00:00:15.979
They give teams a better place to store values than source code, ordinary configuration files, deployment notes, or build logs.

3
00:00:15.979 --> 00:00:20.875
The goal is not just to move a secret; the goal is to control its lifecycle.

4
00:00:20.875 --> 00:00:25.708
Runtime identity patterns reduce the need to distribute static credentials.

5
00:00:25.708 --> 00:00:37.821
Managed identities, workload identities, service principals, and platform identity features can let a running application prove who it is and request only the secrets it is allowed to use.

6
00:00:37.821 --> 00:00:44.006
This keeps the trust relationship closer to the runtime environment and away from copied values.

7
00:00:44.006 --> 00:00:45.295
A .

8
00:00:45.295 --> 00:00:52.575
NET application should retrieve secrets without hardcoding values into source code or ordinary appsettings files.

9
00:00:52.575 --> 00:01:02.820
Configuration can reference the name of a secret, the logical purpose of a secret, or the provider to use, while the actual value remains in a protected store.

10
00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:09.714
For example, the application might reference ExampleSigningKeyName without exposing the signing key itself.

11
00:01:09.714 --> 00:01:11.518
Secret caching needs design.

12
00:01:11.518 --> 00:01:18.863
Caching can reduce latency and dependency pressure, but it affects freshness, rotation behavior, and availability.

13
00:01:18.863 --> 00:01:22.471
A long cache may keep using an old value after rotation.

14
00:01:22.471 --> 00:01:26.208
A short cache may increase dependency on the secret store.

15
00:01:26.208 --> 00:01:31.105
The right choice depends on risk, performance, and operational expectations.

16
00:01:31.105 --> 00:01:36.066
Fail-safe behavior matters when a required secret is missing or inaccessible.

17
00:01:36.066 --> 00:01:48.179
If the application cannot retrieve a required database credential, signing key reference, or integration secret, it should fail clearly rather than silently switching to an unsafe default.

18
00:01:48.179 --> 00:01:53.462
Startup validation can confirm required references without printing secret values.

19
00:01:53.462 --> 00:01:57.328
Secret retrieval logic should be centralized and reviewable.

20
00:01:57.328 --> 00:02:05.382
Scattered calls to secret stores make it hard to understand access patterns, caching, error handling, and audit expectations.

21
00:02:05.382 --> 00:02:11.503
A clear abstraction or configuration layer helps developers use approved patterns consistently.
